# (#126) THE ALL FALLBACK, arm 1: a BARE predicate.
#
# `converged` with no operand reads the runtime's last-observed-slot alias
# (OP_PREDICATE_SLOT), which can be ANY name -- so the query set is not
# statically knowable and the census answers "every name". Every scalar here
# keeps today's full bookkeeping, which is what makes the bare read answer
# about `n`, the last name assigned before it.
#
# This is why #125's NAMED predicate form is the ENABLER for the whole
# analysis rather than a convenience: without it per-name gating is
# undecidable by construction.
x is 100.0
y is 1.0
n is 0
loop while n < 8:
    x is x * 0.5
    y is y + 1.0
    n is n + 1
print of f"x {x} y {y} n {n}"
print of f"bare-converged {converged}"
print of f"bare-stable {stable}"
print of f"bare-diverging {diverging}"
y is y + 1.0
print of f"after-y-converged {converged}"
print of f"after-y-improving {improving}"
